==========================================
SYSADMIN DAY 07 — LOGROTATE
Log Rotation & Retention
==========================================

--- CHECK INSTALL & EXISTING CONFIG ---
which logrotate
cat /etc/logrotate.conf           # global defaults
ls /etc/logrotate.d/               # one config file per service
cat /etc/logrotate.d/<name>          # read a specific one

--- KEY DIRECTIVES ---
daily / weekly / monthly    how often to rotate (time-based)
size 1k / 10M / 1G            rotate once the file reaches this size (instead of/alongside time-based)
rotate N                        keep N old rotated copies, delete anything older
compress                         gzip old rotated copies (saves space)
delaycompress                     compress the PREVIOUS rotation, not the newest one (lets a process
                                   finish any pending writes to the just-rotated file first)
missingok                          don't error if the log file doesn't exist
notifempty                          don't rotate an empty file (nothing to save)
create 0640 user group                after rotating, create a fresh empty file with these permissions
copytruncate                          copy the log, then truncate the ORIGINAL to zero - the process
                                       keeps writing to the same (still-open) file handle, no restart needed
postrotate ... endscript               a block of commands to run AFTER rotation (commonly: tell the
                                       service to reopen its log file, e.g. systemctl reload <service>)

--- DRY RUN (see what WOULD happen, changes nothing) ---
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.d/<name>    # debug mode for one config
sudo logrotate -d /etc/logrotate.conf          # debug mode for everything

--- FORCING A ROTATION NOW (ignores normal schedule) ---
sudo logrotate -f /etc/logrotate.d/<name>

--- TWO WAYS TO ROTATE A LOG A PROCESS STILL HAS OPEN ---
# Method 1: rename + create + signal the process to reopen (typical for well-behaved services)
#    mv app.log app.log.1 ; touch app.log ; kill -HUP <pid-of-service>
#    (needs postrotate/endscript to actually send that signal - see Day 02's lsof lesson:
#     without the signal, the OLD process keeps writing into the renamed/deleted file forever)
#
# Method 2: copytruncate - copy the current content out, then truncate the ORIGINAL file to 0 bytes
#    The process's open file handle is untouched (same file, same inode, just now empty) -
#    no signal needed, works even for programs you can't easily tell to "reopen" its log

--- WHY THIS CONNECTS TO LINUX JOURNAL DAY 02 (lsof) ---
A naive "rm the old log, make a new one" approach is EXACTLY the trap from that day: a process
with the OLD file still open by file descriptor keeps writing into it, invisible and unreachable
by any path, while disk space never gets freed. logrotate's copytruncate (or its proper
rename+signal method) exists specifically to avoid that trap.

